  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 AM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nCurrent water vapor imagery depicts a textbook upper low spinning \nover Arizona. Very diffluent upper flow is present to the east of \nthe low, supporting the widespread precipitation over the \nCentral/Southern Plains and even into the Southern Rockies. Given \nthe deep southerly flow ahead of the low and the absorption of \nHurricane Polo, near record moisture for the time of year is in \nplace across much of the area. TOP's 0z sounding showed 2.05\" of \nPWAT, tied for second highest on record for any sounding after \nSeptember 25.\n\nHere in northeast KS, our initial round of light to moderate rain \ncontinues this morning. Have seen a bit of a recent uptick in rain \nrates within an area of 850 mb frontogenesis north of I-70. Would \nnot be surprised to see some local 1-2\" amounts across this area by \nmid-morning. While there may be a bit of a lull behind the first \nround of activity, expecting the widespread rain over southwest KS \nand the OK/TX Panhandles to lift northeast over the afternoon and \nevening. Coverage should be greatest farther west towards central \nKS, closer to a surface low partially tied to the remnants of Polo. \nGiven the very high moisture and stronger synoptic forcing, would \nnot be surprised to see some isolated spots of 3-6\" rain in a short \nperiod of time across north-central KS. Day 1 ERO from WPC shows a \nmoderate risk of excessive rainfall over portions of north-central \nKS, which matches thinking here very well.\n\nAnd while this flood risk is still the main hazard, also becoming \nincreasingly concerned about a short-lived tornado setup late\nafternoon into the evening. Ahead of the surface low over western \nKS, the LLJ is forecast to increase to around 40-45 kts by 0-3 UTC \ntonight. Coupled with veering low- level winds, effective SRH may \nincrease to around 300 m2s2 with 35-40 kts of effective shear. \nInstability remains the main question given persistent early day \nclouds, but even modest heating would support an uncapped 500-1000 \nJ/kg of ML CAPE. With the high PWAT and very low LCLs, this may favor\nthe development of several mini-supercells capable of producing \ngusty winds and a few tornadoes. The environment looks most favorable\ntowards central KS, though some potential could extend as far east \nas the Highway 75 area. While every day is different, and \nthermodynamics may have been slightly more favorable yesterday \nevening, the presence of multiple supercells and tornadoes in a \nsimilar setup near Amarillo yesterday gives credence to the \npotential.\n\nShifting back to the heavy rain, we may see another brief lull \novernight before rain fills back in Thursday morning. This will \noccur as the weakening upper low moves east across the state and \nascent increases along a southeastward moving cold front. So \ncompared to earlier rounds, this round should see more rain across \neastern portions of the state, especially by the afternoon and \nevening. Have seen a bit more of a southeast trend in recent \nguidance, so have expanded the Flood Watch slightly southeastward. \nStill expecting a general 2-4\" within the watch area, with isolated \nhigher amounts of around 6\". Amounts outside of the watch look \nslightly less, generally 1-3\", but some higher amounts can't be ruled \nout here as well.\n\nMuch less to write about after Thursday night, as drier air behind \nthe front and broad northwesterly flow will keep things quieter. \nTemperatures remain near to slightly below average into early next \nweek. Highs stay mainly in the upper 60s to low 70s, with lows in \nthe 50s to even upper 40s. No notable precipitation chances, \nallowing us to dry out after these very wet next few days.",
